(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["anatomy-exam-1-peripheral-cranial-nerves-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Peripheral & Cranial Nerves — Anatomy Exam 1 — Set 2","category":"Anatomy","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"How many pairs of cranial nerves are there, and where do they emerge from?","choices":["10 pairs; emerge from the spinal cord","12 pairs; emerge equally from the brain and spinal cord","12 pairs; emerge from the brain (not the spinal cord)","31 pairs; emerge from the brain"],"answer":2,"exp":"There are 12 pairs of cranial nerves, which innervate the periphery and emerge from the brain (not the spinal cord), on the ventrolateral surface of the brain, numbered from anterior to posterior (CN I to CN XII). — 6. Peripheral and Cranial Nerves 2026.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"What is the function, origin, and foramen of the olfactory nerve (CN I)?","choices":["A motor nerve controlling eye movement; originating from the mesencephalon; passing through the superior orbital fissure","Sensory (vision); origin: retina of the eye; foramen: optic canal","Sensory (smell); origin: olfactory epithelium; foramen: olfactory foramina within the cribriform plate","Mixed function; origin: pons; foramen: internal acoustic meatus"],"answer":2,"exp":"The olfactory nerve (CN I) has a sensory function (smell), originates from the olfactory epithelium, and passes through the olfactory foramina within the cribriform plate to reach the olfactory bulbs. — 6. Peripheral and Cranial Nerves 2026.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"What is the function, origin, and foramen of the optic nerve (CN II)?","choices":["Motor (extra-ocular muscles); origin: mesencephalon; foramen: superior orbital fissure","Mixed (sensory and motor); origin: pons; foramen: internal acoustic meatus","Sensory (smell); origin: olfactory epithelium; foramen: cribriform plate","Sensory (vision); origin: retina; foramen: optic canal"],"answer":3,"exp":"The optic nerve (CN II) has a sensory function (vision), originates from the retina, and passes through the optic canal, destined for the diencephalon and then the occipital lobe. — 6. Peripheral and Cranial Nerves 2026.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"What does the oculomotor nerve (CN III) control, and where does it originate?","choices":["Controls only the lateral rectus eye muscle; originates from the medulla oblongata region of the brainstem","Controls only the superior oblique muscle of the eye; originates from the pons region of the brainstem","Provides only sensory smell information; originates from the olfactory epithelium of the nose","Controls extra-ocular eye muscles (superior/inferior/medial rectus, inferior oblique, levator palpebrae superioris); originates from the mesencephalon"],"answer":3,"exp":"The oculomotor nerve (CN III) controls the extra-ocular eye muscles — superior rectus, inferior rectus, medial rectus, inferior oblique, and levator palpebrae superioris — and originates from the mesencephalon; it also carries autonomic fibers that synapse in the ciliary ganglion. — 6. Peripheral and Cranial Nerves 2026.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"What does the trochlear nerve (CN IV) control, and through what foramen does it pass?","choices":["Controls the superior oblique eye muscle; passes through the superior orbital fissure","Controls movement of the lateral rectus eye muscle; passes through the optic canal en route to the orbit","Controls the muscles responsible for facial expression; passes through the stylomastoid foramen","Controls several different extra-ocular eye muscles; passes through the jugular foramen"],"answer":0,"exp":"The trochlear nerve (CN IV) controls the superior oblique eye muscle, originating from the mesencephalon and passing through the superior orbital fissure to reach the superior oblique muscle. — 6. Peripheral and Cranial Nerves 2026.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"What are the three divisions of the trigeminal nerve (CN V), and what does each carry?","choices":["Only two divisions exist: ophthalmic (V1) and mandibular (V3), each of which is purely sensory in nature","Ophthalmic, optic, and mandibular divisions, all of them purely sensory in function with no motor fibers at all","Ophthalmic (V1, sensory from forehead/eyelids/nose), maxillary (V2, sensory from lower eyelid/upper lip/cheek), and mandibular (V3, controls mastication)","Three divisions, all of them purely motor in function with no sensory component described whatsoever"],"answer":2,"exp":"The trigeminal nerve (CN V) has mixed sensory and motor function, with three divisions: ophthalmic (V1, sensations from the forehead, eyelids, and nose), maxillary (V2, sensations from the lower eyelid, upper lip, and cheek), and mandibular (V3, controls mastication and also carries sensory information). — 6. Peripheral and Cranial Nerves 2026.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"Through which foramina do the ophthalmic, maxillary, and mandibular divisions of CN V pass, respectively?","choices":["All three divisions pass through the same foramen","Ophthalmic: foramen ovale; Maxillary: superior orbital fissure; Mandibular: foramen rotundum","Ophthalmic: jugular foramen; Maxillary: optic canal; Mandibular: internal acoustic meatus","Ophthalmic: superior orbital fissure; Maxillary: foramen rotundum; Mandibular: foramen ovale"],"answer":3,"exp":"The ophthalmic division passes through the superior orbital fissure, the maxillary division through the foramen rotundum, and the mandibular division through the foramen ovale. — 6. Peripheral and Cranial Nerves 2026.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"What is notable about the trigeminal nerve's size, and where is its semilunar ganglion located?","choices":["It is the largest of the cranial nerves; its sensory branch (the largest branch) contains the semilunar ganglion","It is indeed the largest cranial nerve, though its motor (not sensory) branch is said to contain the semilunar ganglion","It is the smallest of the cranial nerves; it has no associated ganglion at all","It is described as an average-sized cranial nerve; its associated ganglion is named the geniculate ganglion"],"answer":0,"exp":"The trigeminal nerve is the largest of the cranial nerves; its sensory branch, which is the largest branch, contains the semilunar ganglion. — 6. Peripheral and Cranial Nerves 2026.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"What does the abducens nerve (CN VI) innervate, and where does it originate?","choices":["Innervates the muscles of facial expression; originates from the pons","Innervates all extra-ocular muscles; originates from the medulla","Innervates the lateral rectus eye muscle; originates from the pons","Innervates the superior oblique eye muscle; originates from the mesencephalon"],"answer":2,"exp":"The abducens nerve (CN VI) controls eye movements, innervating the lateral rectus eye muscle, and originates from the pons, passing through the superior orbital fissure. — 6. Peripheral and Cranial Nerves 2026.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"What is the sensory versus motor function of the facial nerve (CN VII), and where are its cell bodies located?","choices":["Sensory: taste, with origin from taste buds; Motor: controls facial muscles, originating from the pons; cell bodies located in the geniculate ganglion","Sensory: hearing and balance; Motor: controls tongue movement; cell bodies in the otic ganglion","Purely motor in function, with no sensory component whatsoever; no associated ganglion at all","Sensory function limited to vision; motor function limited to controlling the extra-ocular muscles; cell bodies located in the semilunar ganglion"],"answer":0,"exp":"The facial nerve (CN VII) has a mixed function: sensory (taste, originating from taste buds) and motor (controls muscles of the face, originating from the pons); its cell bodies are located in the geniculate ganglion, and it passes through the internal acoustic meatus. — 6. Peripheral and Cranial Nerves 2026.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"Through which foramen does the facial nerve (CN VII) exit the skull toward the face?","choices":["Through the foramen ovale","Through the optic canal","The stylomastoid foramen","Through the jugular foramen"],"answer":2,"exp":"The facial nerve exits the skull toward the face through the stylomastoid foramen. — 6. Peripheral and Cranial Nerves 2026.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"What is the function of the vestibulocochlear nerve (CN VIII), and what two nerves does it consist of?","choices":["Motor function (eye movement); consists of a single undivided nerve trunk","A sensory function for taste; composed of the greater and lesser petrosal nerve branches","Sensory function (balance and hearing); consists of the vestibular nerve and the cochlear nerve","A mixed function governing swallowing and salivation; composed of branches of the glossopharyngeal nerve"],"answer":2,"exp":"The vestibulocochlear nerve (CN VIII) has a sensory function for balance and hearing, originating from receptors of the vestibule and cochlea, and consists of the vestibular nerve (traveling to the vestibular nuclei of the medulla) and the cochlear nerve (synapsing in the cochlear nuclei of the medulla). — 6. Peripheral and Cranial Nerves 2026.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"What is the mixed function of the glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX)?","choices":["Sensory function: tongue pain (posterior 1/3 of tongue); motor function: swallowing (via salivary gland/pharyngeal muscles)","Sensory function: hearing loss; motor function: controls facial expression muscles","Sensory function limited to vision loss; motor function limited to controlling eye movement","Sensory function: smell perception only; motor function: none present at all"],"answer":0,"exp":"The glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX) has a mixed sensory function (pain sensation from the posterior 1/3 of the tongue, originating there) and motor function (swallowing, with visceral motor to the parotid salivary gland and somatic motor to pharyngeal muscles), passing through the jugular foramen. — 6. Peripheral and Cranial Nerves 2026.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"Where are the sensory neurons of CN IX located?","choices":["In the semilunar ganglion","In the otic ganglion only, with no other sensory ganglion","In the superior and inferior ganglions","In the geniculate ganglion"],"answer":2,"exp":"The sensory neurons of the glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX) are located in the superior and inferior ganglions. — 6. Peripheral and Cranial Nerves 2026.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"What is the sensory versus motor function of the vagus nerve (CN X)?","choices":["Sensory: information from organs; Motor: sends information to organs (respiratory, cardiovascular, and digestive)","Sensory: light and color vision; Motor: controls extrinsic eye muscle movement and pupil constriction","Sensory: balance and hearing information from the inner ear; Motor: none — this nerve is purely sensory","Sensory: taste from the anterior two-thirds of the tongue; Motor: controls the muscles of facial expression"],"answer":0,"exp":"The vagus nerve (CN X) has a sensory function (information from the organs, arriving at autonomic centers of the medulla oblongata) and a motor function (sending information to organs — somatic motor to muscles of the palate/pharynx, and visceral motor to the respiratory, cardiovascular, and digestive organs). — 6. Peripheral and Cranial Nerves 2026.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"Through which foramen does the vagus nerve (CN X) pass, and where are its sensory ganglia located?","choices":["Internal acoustic meatus; sensory neurons in the spiral and vestibular ganglia","Foramen ovale; sensory neurons located in the trigeminal (semilunar) ganglion","Jugular foramen; sensory neurons in the superior and inferior ganglions","Stylomastoid foramen; sensory neurons located in the geniculate ganglion"],"answer":2,"exp":"The vagus nerve passes through the jugular foramen, and its sensory neurons are located in the superior and inferior ganglions. — 6. Peripheral and Cranial Nerves 2026.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"What muscles does the accessory nerve (CN XI) control, and what are its origin and foramen?","choices":["Controls the extra-ocular muscles (superior/inferior/medial rectus, inferior oblique); originates from the mesencephalon, passing through the superior orbital fissure","Controls only the muscles of facial expression; originates from the pons, passing through the internal acoustic meatus and stylomastoid foramen","Controls only the muscles of the tongue (except palatoglossus); originates from the medulla oblongata, passing through the hypoglossal canal","Controls the sternocleidomastoid, trapezius, palate, pharynx, and larynx muscles; originates from the spinal cord and medulla oblongata, passing through the jugular foramen"],"answer":3,"exp":"The accessory nerve (CN XI) controls the sternocleidomastoid, trapezius, palate, pharynx, and larynx muscles, originating from both the spinal cord and medulla oblongata, and passes through the jugular foramen. — 6. Peripheral and Cranial Nerves 2026.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"What is the difference between the internal branch and external branch of the accessory nerve (CN XI)?","choices":["Internal branch innervates muscles of the palate, pharynx, and larynx; external branch innervates the sternocleidomastoid and trapezius muscles","Internal branch innervates the sternocleidomastoid and trapezius muscles; external branch innervates the palate, pharynx, and larynx","Internal branch innervates the sternocleidomastoid only; external branch innervates the trapezius, palate, pharynx, and larynx","Internal branch is purely sensory to the pharynx and larynx; external branch is purely motor to the sternocleidomastoid and trapezius"],"answer":0,"exp":"The internal branch of CN XI innervates the muscles of the palate, pharynx, and larynx, while the external branch innervates the sternocleidomastoid and trapezius muscles. — 6. Peripheral and Cranial Nerves 2026.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"What is the function of the hypoglossal nerve (CN XII), and through what foramen does it pass?","choices":["Motor function, controlling tongue movement; passes through the hypoglossal canal","Mixed sensory/motor function for hearing and balance; passes through the internal acoustic meatus","Sensory function, providing taste from the tongue; passes through the jugular foramen","Motor function, controlling facial expression; passes through the stylomastoid foramen"],"answer":0,"exp":"The hypoglossal nerve (CN XII) has a motor function, controlling tongue movement, originating from the medulla oblongata and passing through the hypoglossal canal to reach the tongue muscles. — 6. Peripheral and Cranial Nerves 2026.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"Which cranial nerve passes through the optic canal?","choices":["Optic nerve (CN II)","Oculomotor nerve (CN III)","Trigeminal nerve, ophthalmic division (CN V1)","Abducens nerve (CN VI)"],"answer":0,"exp":"The optic nerve (CN II) passes through the optic canal. — 6. Peripheral and Cranial Nerves 2026.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"Which three cranial nerves pass through the superior orbital fissure, per the material?","choices":["Olfactory (I), optic (II), and the mandibular division of the trigeminal (V3), along with the facial nerve (VII)","Vagus (X), accessory (XI), and hypoglossal (XII), along with the glossopharyngeal nerve (IX)","Oculomotor (III), trochlear (IV), and abducens (VI), along with the ophthalmic division of the trigeminal (V1)","Facial (VII), vestibulocochlear (VIII), and glossopharyngeal (IX), along with the maxillary division of the trigeminal (V2)"],"answer":2,"exp":"The oculomotor (III), trochlear (IV), and abducens (VI) nerves, along with the ophthalmic division of the trigeminal nerve (V1), all pass through the superior orbital fissure. — 6. Peripheral and Cranial Nerves 2026.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"Which cranial nerves pass through the jugular foramen?","choices":["Facial (VII) and vestibulocochlear (VIII), both via the internal acoustic meatus","Glossopharyngeal (IX), vagus (X), and accessory (XI)","Oculomotor (III) and trochlear (IV), both via the superior orbital fissure","Olfactory (I) and optic (II), via the cribriform plate and optic canal respectively"],"answer":1,"exp":"The glossopharyngeal (IX), vagus (X), and accessory (XI) nerves all pass through the jugular foramen. — 6. Peripheral and Cranial Nerves 2026.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"Which two cranial nerves pass through the internal acoustic meatus?","choices":["Oculomotor (III) and abducens (VI) nerves","Facial (VII) and vestibulocochlear (VIII)","Hypoglossal (XII) and accessory (XI) nerves","Glossopharyngeal (IX) and vagus (X) nerves"],"answer":1,"exp":"The facial nerve (CN VII) and vestibulocochlear nerve (CN VIII) both pass through the internal acoustic meatus. — 6. Peripheral and Cranial Nerves 2026.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"Which cranial nerve is described as consisting of a vestibular nerve traveling to the vestibular nuclei, and a cochlear nerve synapsing in the cochlear nuclei, both of the medulla oblongata?","choices":["Facial nerve (cranial nerve VII)","Vestibulocochlear nerve (CN VIII)","Glossopharyngeal nerve (cranial nerve IX)","Vagus nerve (cranial nerve X)"],"answer":1,"exp":"The vestibulocochlear nerve (CN VIII) consists of the vestibular nerve (traveling to the vestibular nuclei of the medulla oblongata) and the cochlear nerve (synapsing in the cochlear nuclei of the medulla oblongata). — 6. Peripheral and Cranial Nerves 2026.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"Which cranial nerve's motor fibers control muscles of mastication, and which foramen do these motor fibers use?","choices":["Mandibular division of the trigeminal nerve (V3); foramen ovale","Hypoglossal nerve (CN XII); hypoglossal canal, controlling tongue movement","Oculomotor nerve (CN III); superior orbital fissure, controlling eye movement","Facial nerve (CN VII); stylomastoid foramen, controlling facial expression"],"answer":0,"exp":"The mandibular division of the trigeminal nerve (V3) controls the muscles of mastication, with its motor fibers passing through the foramen ovale (its motor nuclei originate in the pons). — 6. Peripheral and Cranial Nerves 2026.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"Which cranial nerve's origin is described as split between sensory (taste buds) and motor (pons), with destination also split between sensory (pons) and motor (muscles of the face)?","choices":["Facial nerve (CN VII)","Glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX)","Vestibulocochlear nerve (CN VIII)","Trigeminal nerve (CN V)"],"answer":0,"exp":"The facial nerve (CN VII) has a sensory origin from taste buds (destination: pons) and a motor origin from the pons (destination: muscles of the face) — a mixed nerve with distinctly split sensory/motor origins and destinations. — 6. Peripheral and Cranial Nerves 2026.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"Which cranial nerve provides both sensory information from the organs and sends motor information to the respiratory, cardiovascular, and digestive organs, exemplifying its extensive visceral role?","choices":["Trigeminal nerve (CN V)","Accessory nerve (CN XI)","Vagus nerve (CN X)","Hypoglossal nerve (CN XII)"],"answer":2,"exp":"The vagus nerve (CN X) provides sensory information from the organs and sends visceral motor information to the respiratory, cardiovascular, and digestive organs, reflecting its extensive parasympathetic visceral role. — 6. Peripheral and Cranial Nerves 2026.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"Which nerve provides innervation to the parotid salivary gland as part of its visceral motor function?","choices":["Trigeminal nerve (CN V)","Glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX)","Hypoglossal nerve (CN XII)","Facial nerve (CN VII), exclusively"],"answer":1,"exp":"The glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX) provides visceral motor innervation to the parotid salivary gland. — 6. Peripheral and Cranial Nerves 2026.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"What ganglion do the oculomotor nerve's autonomic fibers synapse in?","choices":["Geniculate ganglion","Ciliary ganglion","Pterygopalatine ganglion","Otic ganglion"],"answer":1,"exp":"The oculomotor nerve (CN III) has autonomic fibers that synapse in the ciliary ganglion. — 6. Peripheral and Cranial Nerves 2026.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"What structures does the carotid branch of the glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX) monitor?","choices":["The lacrimal gland and nasal mucosa","The tympanic membrane and cochlea","The vocal cords and epiglottis","The carotid body and carotid sinus"],"answer":3,"exp":"The carotid branch of the glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX) monitors the carotid body and carotid sinus. — 6. Peripheral and Cranial Nerves 2026.pptx, Slide 51"}]};
